package com.example.data.sample.notes

val notesEN: Map<String, String> = mapOf(
    // 1. Camino Francés (Last 100 km & Sarria to Santiago)
    "sarria->portomarín" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Traditional starting point of the last 100 kilometers to earn the Compostela. Departure through Rúa Maior in Sarria, passing Magdalena convent and Santa Marina church. It is mandatory to stamp your credential at least twice daily in Galicia; you can do so at the 12th-century Romanesque church of Santiago in Barbadelo and small hostels in Rente, Brea, or Ferreiros. Cross the Belesar reservoir bridge into Portomarín and visit the fortress-church of San Nicolás. Best departure time is around 7:30 AM to walk peacefully.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Pilgrims on specialized websites and forums highlight large crowds early in the morning. On rainy days, traditional mud corridors tend to pool water in As Rozas and A Brea. Sturdy footwear with good grip and walking poles are strongly recommended for the steep descent towards the Belesar reservoir.",

    "portomarín->palas de rei" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart crossing the footbridge over the Miño River, followed by a continuous ascent towards the Ligonde mountain range. At Castromaior, a 400-meter detour to visit the pre-Roman Iron Age hill fort with panoramic views is highly recommended. In Ligonde, admire the 17th-century Lameiros stone cross and the historic pilgrim hospital site. Recommended stops to rest and stamp are Gonzar, Hospital da Cruz, and Ventas de Narón. In Palas de Rei, stamp at San Tirso church.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Steady 8-kilometer climb in the first third of the stage. Pilgrim forums advise that open mountain stretches between Gonzar and Ventas de Narón lack shade; in summer, depart early, wear a hat, and carry fresh water from Portomarín.",

    "palas de rei->arzúa" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Cross the provincial border from Lugo into A Coruña at O Coto. In Leboreiro, pause at Santa María church and cross the medieval bridge over the Seco River with its preserved Roman road. Melide is an essential cultural and culinary stop: sample traditional Galician octopus and stamp at San Pedro church or San Roque chapel next to its 14th-century cross. In Melide, the Primitive Way joins the French Way. The stage ends in Arzúa, renowned for its Arzúa-Ulloa cheese and Santiago church.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Known as a leg-breaker stage due to rolling short steep climbs and stony descents, including the Catasol and Boente stream crossings. Forums suggest pacing your effort, using poles, and taking extra care when crossing the N-547 national road.",

    "arzúa->o pedrouzo" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: A pleasant and peaceful day winding through ancient oak groves (carballeiras), green meadows, and eucalyptus woodlands. Quiet hamlets include Salceda, Brea, Calzada, and Santa Irene, home to a charming baroque hermitage and historic spring where stamping is customary. Arrival in O Pedrouzo (Arca do Pino), the administrative center of O Pino, buzzing with excitement among pilgrims on the eve of reaching Santiago.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Multiple crossings of the busy N-547 road. Specialized pilgrim websites urge using designated pedestrian underpasses and marked crossings. In peak season, hostels in O Pedrouzo fill up quickly; plan ahead or set out before 7:30 AM.",

    "o pedrouzo->santiago de compostela" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: The culmination of the pilgrimage. Depart at dawn through the Amenal woods towards Lavacolla, where medieval pilgrims cleansed themselves in the stream before entering the holy city. Ascend Monte do Gozo for your first emotional view of the Santiago Cathedral towers. Enter on foot through San Lázaro, Porta do Camiño, Praza de Cervantes, and descend through Acibechería into Praza do Obradoiro to the sound of Galician bagpipes. Stamp your credential at the Pilgrim's Reception Office on Rúa Carretas.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: If you plan to attend the 12:00 PM Pilgrim's Mass at the Cathedral, leave O Pedrouzo no later than 6:30 AM. On Monte do Gozo, take the 200-meter detour to the pilgrim monument for the finest panoramic viewpoint over Santiago.",

    // 2. Camino Francés (Saint-Jean to Sarria)
    "saint-jean-pied-de-port->roncesvalles" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart through the Porte d'Espagne in Saint-Jean and climb the legendary Napoleon Route crossing the Bentartea pass and Lepoeder (1,430 m). Reach the Royal Collegiate Church of Roncesvalles, Charlemagne's Silo (Sancti Spiritus), and Santiago chapel. Be sure to stamp at the collegiate hostel and attend the evening pilgrim blessing.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: High-mountain stage with 1,250 meters of elevation gain. If fog, snow, or harsh weather strikes between November and March, the Napoleon Route is closed by law; taking the Valcarlos alternative is mandatory. Carry at least 2 liters of water and energy food; there are no services after Orisson.",

    "roncesvalles->zubiri" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Descend through Navarrese beech and oak forests through the Erro and Esteribar valleys. Stop at Auritz/Burguete and Espinal with their traditional Basque farmhouses. Climb Alto de Mezkiritz and Alto de Erro. Arrive in Zubiri crossing the legendary Rabia Bridge over the Arga River.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: The final descent from Alto de Erro to Zubiri is steep, rocky, and uneven; pilgrim forums warn that wet slate slabs become slippery in damp weather. Walking poles are essential.",

    "zubiri->pamplona" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Walk along the Arga River along tree-lined paths and medieval bridges at Larrasoaña and Irotz. At Trinidad de Arre, cross the Gothic bridge and hermitage over the Ulzama River. Monumental entrance into Pamplona via the Magdalena bridge, Portal de Francia, and Renaissance walls. Visit Santa María Cathedral and Plaza del Castillo.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Gentle stage with good footing. Specialized forums suggest taking the Arga riverbank promenade alternative near Burlada if you prefer a peaceful green entry into the city avoiding asphalt.",

    "pamplona->puente la reina" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Walk through the Ciudadela and ascend the iconic Alto del Perdón (770 m), featuring the famous sculpture where 'the path of the wind crosses that of the stars.' In Puente la Reina, admire the Crucifijo church, Santiago church, and cross the magnificent Romanesque bridge where the French and Aragonese ways converge.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: The descent from Alto del Perdón towards Uterga is very rocky with loose pebbles. Descend carefully with poles to prevent ankle twists frequently reported on forums.",

    "puente la reina->estella" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Pass through Cirauqui, a medieval hilltop village with concentric layout and well-preserved Roman causeway. Cross medieval bridges at Lorca and Villatuerta. In Estella-Lizarra, the 'Toledo of the North,' explore San Pedro de la Rúa church, the Palace of the Kings of Navarre, and Santo Domingo convent.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Rolling day with short continuous slopes. Regular fountains in Mañeru and Cirauqui. In summer, depart early to avoid midday sun across exposed grain fields.",

    "estella->los arcos" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: On leaving Estella, pause at the Monastery of Santa María la Real de Irache and its famous Wine and Water Fountain for a modest tasting and stamp. Pass Azqueta and Villamayor de Monjardín (San Esteban castle). In Los Arcos, visit the stunning Santa María church with its baroque choir and tower.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Between Villamayor de Monjardín and Los Arcos lies a 12-kilometer desolate stretch with no shade or water points. Carry ample water and snacks before leaving Villamayor.",

    "los arcos->logroño" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Walk through olive groves and vineyards towards Torres del Río to visit the 12th-century octagonal Romanesque Holy Sepulchre church. Continue to Viana, where Cesare Borgia is buried at Santa María church. Cross the Stone Bridge over the Ebro River into Logroño, capital of La Rioja, and explore Calle Laurel and the Co-Cathedral of Santa María de la Redonda.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Steady undulating trail. In Torres del Río, take time to visit the Holy Sepulchre interior. Rest and hydrate before entering the lively tapas streets of Logroño.",

    "logroño->nájera" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart through Grajera Natural Park along the reservoir. Walk through Navarrete, famous for its pottery tradition and the baroque church of La Asunción. Pass the ruins of San Juan de Acre pilgrim hospital. Enter Nájera, cradle of Navarrese kings, and visit Santa María la Real monastery with its royal pantheon and knights' cloister.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Gentle stage along dirt tracks between vineyards. Little shade on the final stretch into Nájera; carry sun protection and water.",

    "nájera->santo domingo de la calzada" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Rolling stage through grain and vineyard fields via Azofra. Ascend to Cirueña with its high plateau. Arrive in Santo Domingo de la Calzada, founded by Saint Dominic to aid pilgrims. Tour the cathedral with its live rooster and hen cage, commemorating the famous miracle of the hanged pilgrim.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: High plateau exposed to strong wind and sun. Stock up on water in Azofra as Cirueña has limited services during off-peak hours.",

    "santo domingo de la calzada->belorado" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Leave La Rioja and enter the province of Burgos (Castile and León) at Redecilla del Camino, home to a magnificent 12th-century Romanesque baptismal font. Pass Castildelgado and Viloria de Rioja (birthplace of Santo Domingo). Conclude in Belorado, visiting San Pedro and Santa María churches and its arcaded main square.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Flat trail along agricultural tracks parallel to the N-120 road. Multiple rest areas with water in intermediate villages.",

    "belorado->san juan de ortega" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Ascend through the Montes de Oca through thick oak and pine forests via Tosantos and Villafranca Montes de Oca. Historic climb to Puerto de la Pedraja (1,150 m) with its monument to civil war victims. Arrive at the peaceful Monastery of San Juan de Ortega, celebrated for the Romanesque miracle of the light on the Annunciation capital.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Isolated mountain forest stretch between Villafranca and San Juan de Ortega (over 12 km without villages). Carry adequate food and warm clothes as temperatures drop in the forest.",

    "san juan de ortega->burgos" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Pass through Agés and Atapuerca, globally renowned for its UNESCO prehistoric archaeological sites. Ascend Alto de Matagrande with expansive views over the Burgos valley. Enter Burgos along the scenic Arlanzón river path towards the French Gothic masterpiece of Santa María Cathedral.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Pilgrim forums recommend following the riverbank path (Castañares-Arlanzón) into Burgos to avoid the industrial park.",

    "burgos->hornillos del camino" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Step onto the legendary Castilian Meseta. Leave Burgos via Tardajos and Rabé de las Calzadas, descending into the wide horizon of cereal plateaus and silence. Arrive in Hornillos del Camino, a quintessential linear pilgrim village along the valley floor.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Completely open terrain without shade. In summer, early morning departure is vital to beat the scorching heat. In winter, strong winds require windproof gear.",

    "hornillos del camino->castrojeriz" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Walk across the endless plains through San Bol and Hontanas, a hidden gem nestled in a ravine. Pass beneath the monumental Gothic arches of San Antón monastery ruins, where Antonine monks cured the sacred fire. Arrive in Castrojeriz beneath its hilltop castle fortress.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: The sun-drenched approach through San Antón is breathtaking. Stamp at the monastery ruins and replenish water in Hontanas.",

    "castrojeriz->frómista" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Steep climb up Alto de Mostelares (860 m) rewarding you with immense 360-degree views of the Meseta. Cross the Pisuerga River over the medieval Fitero Bridge into the province of Palencia. Pass the historic San Nicolás hermitage. Follow the tranquil Castile Canal towpath into Frómista, home to San Martín church, the pinnacle of Spanish Romanesque architecture.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: The climb up Mostelares is intense but short; descend with care on the gravel. Walking alongside the Castile Canal offers pleasant, flat relief.",

    "frómista->carrión de los condes" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Walk along the pilgrim track through Población de Campos, Villovieco, and Villalcázar de Sirga, where you must visit the fortified Templar church of Santa María la Blanca. Arrive in Carrión de los Condes, exploring San Zoilo monastery with its plateresque cloister and Santa María del Camino church.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Mostly flat route beside the road or via the Ucieza river scenic alternative through Villovieco. Villalcázar is great for a mid-stage rest.",

    "carrión de los condes->ledigos" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: The true heart of the Roman Via Traiana. A straight 17-kilometer Roman road across the desolate plateau between Carrión and Calzadilla de la Cueza, without a single settlement or fountain. Continue through Ledigos with its church honoring Saint James.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Crucial stage for preparation. Carry at least 2.5 liters of water, energy food, and sun protection. Start before dawn to cross the open stretch in cool weather.",

    "ledigos->sahagún" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Pass through Terradillos de los Templarios and Moratinos, known for its traditional wine cellars dug into clay hills. Cross from Palencia into León. Arrive in Sahagún, the 'Romanesque Mudéjar capital,' and visit San Tirso, San Lorenzo, and the pilgrim sanctuary of La Peregrina.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Gentle, undulating day through small agricultural villages with handy services. Visit the pilgrim sanctuary in Sahagún for a special stamp.",

    "sahagún->el burgo ranero" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart crossing the Cuaresma bridge and choose the Calzada de los Hermanillos or the Real Camino Francés via Bercianos del Real Camino to El Burgo Ranero, lined with rows of shade trees planted for pilgrims along the trail.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: The path along Bercianos features water fountains and rest areas. Keep a steady pace on this straight, meditative stretch.",

    "el burgo ranero->mansilla de las mulas" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Continue along the tree-lined path through Reliegos, renowned for its lively pilgrim bars and clay cellars. Reach Mansilla de las Mulas, crossing the Esla River and admiring its preserved medieval defensive walls and gate.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Easy, flat walking. Stop in Reliegos for refreshments before entering Mansilla.",

    "mansilla de las mulas->león" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Cross the Porma River at Villarente and climb Portillo hill for the first panorama of León. Cross the Torío River into the historic center of León. Marvel at the Pulchra Leonina (Cathedral of León) with its world-famous stained glass windows, San Isidoro collegiate church, and Gaudí's Casa Botines.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Urban approach from Puente Castro into the city center. Allocate ample afternoon time to explore the cathedral and the Barrio Húmedo tapas area.",

    "león->san martín del camino" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Exit León passing San Marcos convent, heading towards Virgen del Camino sanctuary. Follow the agricultural route through Valverde de la Virgen towards San Martín del Camino.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Many pilgrims prefer the scenic southern alternative via Villar de Mazarife for quieter rural tracks instead of walking alongside the N-120 road.",

    "san martín del camino->astorga" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Reach Hospital de Órbigo and cross its magnificent 300-meter medieval bridge (Passo Honroso of knight Suero de Quiñones). Gentle ascent to Santo Toribio cross overlooking Astorga. Explore Astorga: Cathedral of Santa María, Gaudí's Episcopal Palace, Roman walls, and taste traditional cocido maragato.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Fantastic historic stop at Hospital de Órbigo. The view of Astorga and the Teleno mountain from Santo Toribio cross is unforgettable.",

    "astorga->rabanal del camino" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Enter the magical Maragatería region with its red-stone villages. Pass through Castrillo de los Polvazares (declared historic site), Santa Catalina de Somoza, and El Ganso. Climb to Rabanal del Camino, where Benedictine monks sing Gregorian chant at nightfall.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Gradual climb towards the Montes de León. Wear sturdy shoes as stones increase on the trail. Attend the evening blessing in Rabanal.",

    "rabanal del camino->ponferrada" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Epic mountain stage. Climb to Foncebadón and the legendary Cruz de Ferro (Iron Cross, 1,500 m) to leave your symbolic stone. Pass Manjarín hermitage and begin a steep mountain descent to Acebo, Riego de Ambrós, and Molinaseca with its Roman bridge. Conclude in Ponferrada at the impressive Templar Castle.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Steep, rocky descent from Cruz de Ferro to Molinaseca with significant negative elevation gain. Use walking poles and watch your footing.",

    "ponferrada->villafranca del bierzo" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Walk through the fertile Bierzo valley, dotted with vineyards, cherry trees, and orchards. Pass through Camponaraya and Cacabelos on the Cúa River. Arrive in Villafranca del Bierzo, 'Little Compostela,' home to the Santiago church with its famous Pardon Gate (Puerta del Perdón).\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Mostly flat terrain along country lanes between vineyards. Sample Bierzo wine and rest well before tomorrow's mountain ascent.",

    "villafranca del bierzo->o cebreiro" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: The mythical climb into Galicia. Follow the Valcarce valley through Trabadelo, La Portela, and Ambasmestas to Las Herrerías. Face the strenuous ascent through La Faba and Laguna de Castilla into O Cebreiro (1,300 m) with its thatched pallozas and pre-Romanesque Holy Grail church.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Demanding mountain climb gaining 900 meters in the final 8 km from Las Herrerías. Pace yourself, hydrate, and prepare for rapid weather shifts at O Cebreiro summit.",

    "o cebreiro->triacastela" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: High mountain ridge walk through Galicia with panoramic sea-of-clouds vistas. Pass the Pilgrim Monument at Alto de San Roque and Alto do Poio (1,337 m). Gentle descent through Fonfría and Biduedo down into Triacastela, nestled in the valley.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Rolling alpine terrain with breezy conditions. Early morning clouds often linger; wear warm windproof layers.",

    "triacastela->sarria" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Choice of two historic paths: through the scenic valley of Samos to visit its monumental Benedictine monastery, or via the shorter rural trail of San Xil among ancient oak woods. Both routes converge in Sarria.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: The Samos variant adds about 5 km but visits one of Spain's oldest and grandest monastic complexes. Both routes are visually splendid.",

    // 3. Camino Primitivo (13 stages)
    "oviedo->grado" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: The oldest pilgrimage route begins at San Salvador Cathedral in Oviedo ('Whoever goes to Santiago and not to the Savior honors the servant and forgets the Lord'). Walk through San Lázaro de Paniceres, cross the Nora River over the Gallegos bridge, and continue through Escamplero. Descend to the Nalón River and enter Grado with its traditional market and historic architecture.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Rolling Asturian profile with moderate climbs and descents along rural paths and quiet roads. Stamp your credential at Oviedo Cathedral before setting out.",

    "grado->salas" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart Grado following the Cubia River and face the climb to Alto del Fresno with its 16th-century hermitage offering scenic views. Descend to San Marcelo and Cornellana, visiting the 11th-century Monastery of San Salvador. Follow the Nonaya River along a shady path to Salas, exploring Valdés-Salas castle and palace.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Steady climb to Fresno pass and shaded, humid forest trail into Salas. Visit Cornellana monastery and stock up on provisions in Salas.",

    "salas->tineo" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Strenuous climb up the Nonaya valley towards Alto de la Espina and La Pereda, where medieval leper colonies stood. Walk through lush Asturian pastoral countryside with cattle grazing on rolling hills. Arrive in Tineo, resting at the San Francisco monastery and church of San Pedro.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Significant climb out of Salas through rocky forest trails. In rainy weather, mud is common; sturdy waterproof boots and poles are highly recommended.",

    "tineo->pola de allande" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Beautiful journey past San Félix hermitage, through Obona forest with its historic monastery. Continue through El Pedregal and along the crest of Sierra de Tineo with expansive valley views, descending into Pola de Allande overlooked by Cienfuegos Palace.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Rural mountain paths with scattered services. The detour to Obona monastery is rich in history. Rest well in Pola de Allande ahead of the Palo Pass ascent.",

    "pola de allande->la mesa" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: The queen stage of the Primitive Way. Ascend through dramatic landscapes to the summit of Puerto del Palo (1,146 m), enjoying sweeping vistas of the Cantabrian mountains. Pass through Montefurado and descend to the high village of La Mesa.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Strenuous high-mountain stage. Weather can change abruptly with thick fog or wind. Take the Hospitales route only in clear weather and good physical condition.",

    "la mesa->grandas de salime" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Breathtaking descent to the massive Salime reservoir dam across the Navia River. Ascend through oak and pine woodlands to Grandas de Salime, visiting the collegiate church of San Salvador and the renowned ethnographic museum.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Steep, prolonged descent of over 700 meters to the dam wall that tests the knees. Use walking poles and take steady, controlled steps.",

    "grandas de salime->a fonsagrada" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Cross from Asturias into Galicia by climbing to Alto del Acebo (1,030 m). Welcoming Galician stone milestones appear as you enter A Fonsagrada, legendary town named after its holy fountain (Fons Sacrata).\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Continuous mountain climb into Galicia. In A Fonsagrada, sample the famous mountain octopus and fill your water supply.",

    "a fonsagrada->o cádavo" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Route through Montouto, passing the historic Royal Hospital ruins and megalithic dolmen on the mountain crest. Descend through Paradavella to O Cádavo (Baleira).\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Mountain trails with short steep ramps. The historic hospital site at Montouto is a magical high-altitude spot to pause and reflect.",

    "o cádavo->lugo" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Climb Alto de Vaqueriza and walk through Vilabade with its gothic church of Santa María. Enter Lugo through the historic gateway of the UNESCO Roman Wall, the only fully intact Roman wall in the world. Visit Santa María Cathedral and walk along the top of the Roman wall.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Long but mostly downhill and gentle stage. Spend the evening circling the city along the wall ramparts and enjoying local tapas.",

    "lugo->san romao" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Exit Lugo through Porta Miñá, crossing the Miño River over the Roman bridge. Quiet, level walking through traditional rural hamlets including San Vicente do Burgo and San Romao da Retorta, following traces of the Roman road.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Peaceful rural day with flat terrain through shaded corridors. Limited dining options; carry some snacks and water.",

    "san romao->melide" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Walk through the tranquil Galician countryside through Ferreira and As Seixas, passing the Careón mountain range. Reach Melide, where the Primitive Way joins the French Way, bringing together pilgrims from both routes.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Moderate stage through eucalyptus groves. In Melide, treat yourself to traditional pulpo á feira at local pulperías.",

    "melide->arzúa" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Combined route through Boente with its Santiago church and Ribadiso do Baixo with its picture-perfect medieval bridge over the Iso River. Climb into Arzúa, famous for its denomination cheese and vibrant pilgrim atmosphere.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Rolling hills with several short sharp dips. Rest by the river at Ribadiso before tackling the final climb into Arzúa.",

    "arzúa->santiago de compostela" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: The final triumphal walk towards Santiago de Compostela. Pass through Salceda, Santa Irene with its hermitage, O Pedrouzo, and Lavacolla. Ascend Monte do Gozo and enter the holy city to celebrate in Praza do Obradoiro.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Long 39-kilometer push if done in one day, or split naturally at O Pedrouzo. Depart before dawn to arrive in Santiago for early celebrations.",

    // 4. Camino del Norte (Galicia stages)
    "ribadeo->lourenzá" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Welcome to the Galician North Way. Depart from the maritime town of Ribadeo, passing through Ove and ascending into the green hills of Vilanova de Lourenzá, home to the Baroque Monastery of San Salvador and famous faba beans.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Steady ascent leaving the coast towards inland valleys. Carry water and sample local beans in Lourenzá.",

    "lourenzá->abadín" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Ascend through Mondoñedo, one of Galicia's historic capitals. Visit the stunning Cathedral of the Assumption, the fountain of A Vella, and taste King's Tart. Climb the challenging pass into Terra Chá at Abadín.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Demanding mountain climb out of Mondoñedo. Take time to explore the cathedral in Mondoñedo before starting the climb.",

    "abadín->vilalba" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Walk across the wide, level plains of Terra Chá through agricultural hamlets and green pastures. Arrive in Vilalba, crowned by the medieval Octagonal Tower of the Andrade castle (now a Parador).\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Easy, flat walking along rural tracks and farm roads. Very pleasant on the joints after previous climbs.",

    "vilalba->baamonde" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Gentle walking through oak woods and pastoral landscapes alongside the Ladra River. In Baamonde, visit the 9th-century church of Santiago and the museum of sculptor Víctor Corral, including his carved chestnut tree.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Flat, peaceful stage with plenty of shade. Great stage to walk at a relaxed pace.",

    "baamonde->sobrado dos monxes" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Walk through dense forests into the province of A Coruña, reaching the monumental Cistercian Monastery of Santa María de Sobrado dos Monxes, with its cloister of the Medals and serene lagoon.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Long rural stage with few services. Sleeping inside the historic monastery hostel is an unforgettable pilgrim experience.",

    "sobrado dos monxes->arzúa" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart Sobrado past the medieval monastic lagoon through Boimorto and Sendelle, visiting the 12th-century Santa María church. Connect with the French Way in Arzúa.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Scenic rural tracks through agricultural Galicia. Arzúa offers full pilgrim amenities and great local cheeses.",

    // 5. Camino Portugués Central (6 stages)
    "tui->o porriño" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart from the fortified Cathedral of Santa María in Tui, towering over the Miño River. Walk through the historic Jewish quarter and San Telmo chapel. Follow the Louro riverbank alternate trail to avoid the industrial park, arriving in O Porriño with its distinctive Town Hall designed by Antonio Palacios.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Specialized websites and pilgrim sources strongly advise taking the river Louro path (Senda do Río Louro) at the outskirts of O Porriño to bypass the long industrial area on asphalt.",

    "o porriño->redondela" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Gentle climb through Mos, visiting Santa Eulalia church and the Pazo de Mos. Ascend to Enxertade and descend to the coastal town of Redondela on the Vigo estuary, famous for its historic railway viaducts and Santiago church.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Short climb over Alto de Enxertade followed by a steep descent into Redondela. In Redondela, taste local oysters and seafood.",

    "redondela->pontevedra" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Scenic coastal views over the Ría de Vigo and San Simón island. Walk through Arcade, famed for its oysters, and cross the historic medieval bridge of Ponte Sampaio over the Verdugo River, site of the Napoleonic battle. Enter Pontevedra and visit the scallop-shaped Pilgrim Virgin Chapel (Santuario da Virxe da Peregrina).\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Steep climb up the ancient Roman military road (Brea Vella da Canicouva) after Ponte Sampaio. Uneven stone slabs can be slippery when wet.",

    "pontevedra->caldas de reis" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart across the Lérez River over O Burgo bridge. Peaceful walk through Albariño vineyards, stone granaries (hórreos), and rural villages in the Salnés valley. In Caldas de Reis, soak your feet in the warm thermal waters of As Burgas fountain and cross the Roman bridge over the Umia River. Visit Santo Tomás Becket church.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Forums note that near Barro you can access the Rio Barosa Natural Park from this stage or the start of the next. Mostly flat terrain along pleasant country lanes.",

    "caldas de reis->padrón" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: An essential and unmissable stop at the Río Barosa Natural Park (A Barosa), located a short distance off the path, to admire its spectacular stepped waterfalls and traditional complex of fourteen historic watermills. Visit Santa María de Iria Flavia church, ancient episcopal seat, and in Padrón, the church of Santiago preserving O Pedrón (the Roman altar stone where, according to tradition, the disciples moored the stone boat carrying Saint James's body). Taste authentic Padrón peppers and visit Rosalía de Castro's house museum.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Pilgrim sources and specialized forums emphasize that the detour to the Barosa waterfall is entirely worthwhile, offering peaceful shade and a café to recharge. The gentle climb to Santa Mariña de Carracedo winds through cool, shaded oak forests.",

    "padrón->santiago de compostela" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Pass through A Escravitude sanctuary with its miraculous spring, where stamping is customary. From O Milladoiro, catch your first distant glimpse of the cathedral spires. Enter Santiago through Porta Faxeira into Rúa do Franco, arriving triumphantly in Praza do Obradoiro before the Cathedral facade. Collect your Compostela at the Pilgrim's Reception Office on Rúa Carretas.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Moderate climbing with several urban stretches. Start early to avoid traffic on the approaches to Santiago and allow sufficient time at the Pilgrim's Office.",

    // 6. Camino Inglés (6 stages - Historic Route)
    "ferrol->neda" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: This stage follows the traditional Historic Route. The trail begins at Curuxeiras dock (km 0 of the English Way) in Ferrol, where medieval pilgrims from northern Europe arrived by ship. Stamp at the dock information point and San Julián Co-Cathedral. The Historic Route skirts the Ferrol estuary along coastal paths and traditional rural lanes, avoiding the provincial highway shoulders and industrial estates of modern diversions. In Neda, do not miss Santa María church and its famous Neda bread baked with Belelle river water. Best departure time is around 8:00 AM to stroll peacefully along the shoreline.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Pilgrims on specialized websites and forums highlight that the Historic Route along the water is far safer and more scenic than walking beside road traffic. Beware of high tides on small riverside passages and wear comfortable footwear for hard surfaces in the opening third.",

    "neda->pontedeume" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: This stage follows the traditional Historic Route. The path climbs through neighborhood tracks and traditional dirt lanes, avoiding heavy traffic on the N-651 around Fene and Cabanas. Cross the stone bridge over the Eume River and ascend into the medieval quarter of Pontedeume. Visit Santiago church and view the Andrade tower. Stamp at Neda hostel or San Martiño de Porto church in Cabanas.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Specialized websites and forums note that the Historic Route features steep short ramps in Fene and O Vilar but avoids dangerous highway crossings. When descending towards Cabanas beach, pavement may be slippery in rain or morning dew.",

    "pontedeume->betanzos" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: This stage follows the traditional Historic Route. This historic alignment winds through hillside trails and dirt tracks in Boebre and Miño, steering clear of long asphalt diversions and highway overpasses introduced in recent roadworks. Stop at the Miño estuary and cross the medieval bridge over the Lambre River. In Betanzos de los Caballeros, explore its three medieval town gates, the Gothic church of Santa María del Azogue, and savor the famous Betanzos Spanish omelet. Stamp at San Francisco church.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Leg-breaker stage with continuous short steep ascents and descents. On forums, hikers highlight the steep initial climb out of Pontedeume towards O Monte and the stony drop into Miño. Walking poles are strongly recommended.",

    "betanzos->hospital de bruma" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: This stage follows the traditional Historic Route. The historic alignment climbs through ancient mountain corridors and native woodlands in Vilacova and Presedo, avoiding land consolidation gravel tracks and exposed asphalt roads. This is the queen stage due to accumulated elevation gain and solitude. Stamp at Presedo hostel or San Esteban de Cos church. In Hospital de Bruma, visit the site of the medieval pilgrim hospital founded in 1140.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Forums emphasize departing very early and carrying adequate water and food, as there are almost no open services between Presedo and As Travesas. The Historic Route provides deeper shade on the steep climb but may hold mud in damp spots.",

    "hospital de bruma->sigüeiro" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: This stage follows the traditional Historic Route. It winds through woodland dirt trails and traditional pathways among oaks and eucalyptus instead of endless straight sections of regional asphalt. Mostly flat and gently descending terrain through the municipality of Ordes. Visit San Julián de Poulo church and stamp at its nearby tavern. In Sigüeiro, enjoy the river park beside the Tambre River and its medieval bridge.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Specialized websites and forums warn of few working water fountains in the first 12 kilometers; fill your canteen in Bruma. The soft earth of the Historic Route offers welcome relief to tired joints.",

    "sigüeiro->santiago de compostela" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: This stage follows the traditional Historic Route. Follow the historic route entering Santiago through the Sionlla forest and Tambre river valley, minimizing walking through the Tambre industrial estate via green historical corridors. Depart early to reach Santiago before noon. Cross the medieval bridge over the Tambre River. Enter through Porta do Camiño towards Praza de Cervantes, Acibechería, and the magnificent Praza do Obradoiro. Stamp at the Pilgrim's Reception Office for your Compostela.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Pilgrims in forums note that approaching along the Historic Route makes entering the city far more pleasant and natural. Watch for traffic near San Caetano and enjoy every step of the final kilometers.",

    // 7. Vía de la Plata (Selected key stages)
    "sevilla->guillena" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart from the majestic Seville Cathedral across Triana Bridge. Walk through Camas and Santiponce, visiting the Roman archaeological site of Itálica with its amphitheater and mosaics. Stamp at San Isidoro del Campo monastery. Reach Guillena across the Rivera de Huelva river.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Flat stage intensely exposed to Andalusian sun. Start at first dawn light and carry plenty of water; shade is virtually non-existent.",

    "mérida->alcuéscar" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Leave Roman Mérida past the Milagros Aqueduct and the ancient Proserpina reservoir. Walk across Extremaduran dehesas through El Carrascalejo and Aljucén. In Alcuéscar, visit the 7th-century Visigothic basilica of Santa Lucía del Trampal.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Marathon 36-kilometer stage with very few services across 20 km of open dehesa. Carry ample water and food.",

    "salamanca->el cubo de la tierra del vino" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Exit Salamanca across the northern plateau through Calzada de Valdunciel with its Roman milestones. Enter Zamora province at El Cubo de la Tierra del Vino, home to ancient vines and underground wine cellars.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Long 36-kilometer agricultural track with little shade. Carry ample water.",

    "zamora->riego del camino" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Exit Zamora heading across the grain plateau towards Montamarta beside the Esla reservoir and the hermitage of Virgen del Castillo. Continue to Riego del Camino.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Check reservoir water levels in Montamarta; during high water, use the signposted road alternative.",

    "granja de moreruela->tábara" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Junction of the Vía de la Plata and the Sanabrés Way. Cross the Esla River over Quintos Bridge into Tábara, home to the church of Santa María and Mozarabic scriptorium center.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Solitary scrubland trail. After Quintos bridge, the sandy path climbs gently. Carry water from Granja.",

    // 8. Camino Sanabrés
    "ourense->cea" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Exit Ourense across the Miño River and face a steep climb through Cudeiro. Reach San Cristovo de Cea, renowned for its wood-fired artisan bread (Pan de Cea). Optional detour to the monumental Oseira Monastery.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Very steep 5-kilometer climb out of Ourense. Forums strongly recommend the variant to Oseira Monastery to sleep in its historic guesthouse.",

    "outeiro->santiago de compostela" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Final stretch of the Sanabrés Way. Panoramic views of Pico Sacro hill. Enter Santiago through Angrois and Porta de Mazarelos into Praza do Obradoiro.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Short, emotional stage. Arrive early to attend the pilgrim mass.",

    // 11. Camino de San Salvador
    "león->la robla" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart from León Cathedral following the Bernesga River towards the Cantabrian foothills. Arrive in the mining town of La Robla with Celada hermitage.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Flat transition stage along river paths and railway lines. Fill water in Cuadros.",

    "pajares->pola de lena" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Steep descent down Pajares valley among chestnut and beech groves. Must-see 500-meter detour at Campomanes to Santa Cristina de Lena (9th century), pre-Romanesque jewel. Arrive in Pola de Lena.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: The descent drops over 1,000 meters and taxes the knees heavily. Use poles and descend slowly.",

    "pola de lena->oviedo" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Walk through the Asturian mining valleys, Olloniego medieval bridge and palace, ascending El Padrún to enter Oviedo at San Salvador Cathedral.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Rolling path combining green trails and urban approaches. Collect your Salvadorana certificate at the cathedral.",

    // 12. Finisterre y Muxía
    "santiago de compostela->negreira" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Depart Praza do Obradoiro down Rúa das Hortas towards Augapesada. Climb Alto do Mar de Ovellas and cross magical Ponte Maceira over the Tambre River, one of Spain's prettiest villages. Arrive in Negreira.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Steep climb after Augapesada on stone paths. Depart around 8:00 AM to enjoy the morning light in Ponte Maceira.",

    "negreira->olveiroa" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: The most solitary, deep rural Galician stage through Vilaserío and Santa Mariña to the restored stone village of Olveiroa.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Long 33-kilometer stage with limited intermediate services. Stock up on food and water.",

    "olveiroa->cee" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Pass Castrelo reservoir to Hospital, where the route splits to Fisterra or Muxía. Climb Alto das Cruces for your first stunning view of the Atlantic Ocean, descending into Corcubión and Cee.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: The first view of the ocean is deeply moving. Follow yellow markers carefully at the Hospital junction.",

    "cee->fisterra" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Coastal walk past Estorde beach and along the long white sands of Langosteira beach. Reach Fisterra village and climb the final 3 km to Cape Finisterre Lighthouse (km 0), Roman End of the World. Watch the Atlantic sunset and collect the Fisterrana.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Short stage designed to be savored. Please do not burn clothes or shoes at the lighthouse; enjoy the sunset respecting nature.",

    "fisterra->muxía" to "RECOMMENDATIONS: Coastal trail between Fisterra and Muxía via Lires with its estuary sunset. Enter Muxía towards Punta da Barca sanctuary facing crashing ocean waves and the balancing stones (Pedra de Abalar). Collect the Muxiana.\nLAST-MINUTE TIPS: Rugged coastal ups and downs through forests and cliffs. Exercise great caution near high waves at the sanctuary rocks."
)
